<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Crécy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1346">1346</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Crécy · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Crécy was fought on 26 August 1346 near Crécy-en-Ponthieu in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, in a defensive position, defeated a much larger French army under Philip VI, as English and Welsh longbowmen broke repeated French cavalry charges. Many French nobles died, among them the blind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and Edward went on to besiege Calais.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy.jpg" alt="Battle of Crécy" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Loyset Liédet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_crecy_froissart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>English monarchs had held lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066, which made them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the kings of France. On 24 May 1337 the Great Council of Philip VI in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> agreed that Edward III's lands in France should be taken back into the king's hands because he had breached his obligations as a vassal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> began. Eight years of costly and inconclusive fighting followed. In 1345 Henry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>, led a rapid campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, defeating two large French armies at Bergerac and Auberoche. Edward landed at Saint-Vaast-la-Hougue in Normandy on 12 July 1346, achieved complete strategic surprise and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> on 26 July, where more than 5,000 French soldiers and civilians were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The English burned a path to within 2 miles (3 km) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, then marched north hoping to meet an allied Flemish army. Edward chose a sloping hillside facing south-east at Crécy-en-Ponthieu, with Wadicourt anchoring the left flank and Crécy and the River Maye protecting the right. His men dug pits in front of their positions and set up several primitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons. The English army, estimated by modern historians at between 7,000 and 15,000, was substantially smaller than the French, which Clifford Rogers puts at twice to three times its size.</p><p>Late on 26 August the French advanced, unfurling the oriflamme, the sacred banner that meant no prisoners would be taken. A rainstorm broke. Genoese crossbowmen engaged the longbowmen, who outranged them and shot more than three times as fast, and the crossbowmen fled. The French then charged uphill in a series of cavalry attacks, disordered by the fleeing crossbowmen, the mud and the pits, and broken up by archery that killed or wounded many of the unarmoured horses. By the time the charges reached the dismounted English men-at-arms they had lost much of their impetus. They continued late into the night, each ending in fierce fighting and a French retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>English losses were very low; a roll-call after the battle gave about forty dead, though some modern historians think around three hundred. The English heralds counted 1,542 bodies of French noble men-at-arms on the field, perhaps not including those killed the following day, and no count was made of lower-born foot soldiers. Edward then besieged Calais, which fell after eleven months and stayed in English hands until 1558. The battle was reported to the English parliament on 13 September as a sign of divine favour. Rogers calls it a total English victory, and Andrew Ayton says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s reputation as a military power was established in an evening's hard fighting. It confirmed the longbow as a dominant weapon of Western European warfare.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Crécy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Raoul was killed fighting for France at the <b>Battle of Crécy</b> in 1346, and in 1431 René of Anjou, who claimed Lorraine through his wife Isabella, was defeated and captured at the Battle of Bulgnéville by his rival Antoine de Vaudémont with Burgundian help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hundred Years’ War against France, English armies combining longbowmen with dismounted men-at-arms won famous victories at the <b>Battle of Crécy</b> in 1346, the Battle of Poitiers in 1356 and the Battle of Agincourt in 1415, and Henry V conquered Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hundred Years' War with England (1337–1453) began disastrously: English archers destroyed the French cavalry at the <b>Battle of Crécy</b> in 1346, King John II was captured at the Battle of Poitiers in 1356, and defeat at the Battle of Agincourt followed in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Genoese crossbowmen served as mercenaries across Europe, notably for France at <b>Crécy</b> in 1346, and Genoese admirals commanded the fleets of foreign kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>English archers and dismounted men-at-arms cut down repeated French cavalry charges at <b>Crécy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The surrender concluded the <b>Crécy</b> campaign, a series of large-scale raids across northern France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
